Stop reading past the module list when naming a Windows process.
To find the applet name and command line of another busybox process,
get_bb_string fetches the process's modules into a 32-entry array and
looks for the one named after its executable. EnumProcessModules
reports the size needed for all modules, not the size it filled, and
the search ran to that size. For a process with more than 32 modules,
as most Windows programs have, a search that found no module by that
name read past the end of the array on the stack and passed whatever it
found there to GetModuleFileNameExA as module handles. This could
happen in ps, pgrep, pidof and killall.

Limit the search to the entries that were filled.

---
 win32/process.c |    3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/win32/process.c
+++ b/win32/process.c
@@ -643,6 +643,9 @@
 	if (!EnumProcessModules(proc, mlist, sizeof(mlist), &needed)) {
 		goto finish;
 	}
+	/* needed may exceed the size of mlist: only that much was filled */
+	if (needed > sizeof(mlist))
+		needed = sizeof(mlist);
 
 	for (i=0; exe != NULL && i<needed/sizeof(HMODULE); ++i) {
 		char modname[MAX_PATH];
